  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.2. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to strengthen policies and strategies to promote and ensure equal opportunities, participation and treatment between women and men, including equal remuneration for work of equal value</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.The Confederation of Employers of Ukraine developed and adopted a gender-responsive workplace policy in March 2021. This tool enables enterprises to assess their compliance with gender equality related national laws and regulations. It was subsequently complemented, endorsed and officially launched by the Government Commissioner for Gender Equality Policy.

2.The Ministry of Social Policy of Government of Ukraine developed and issued methodological recommendations for gender audits in private enterprises
 The ILO, through ILO Project on Improvement of Wages System in Ukraine provided on-going technical support and guidance on the content of the gender-responsive workplace policy for alignment with international labour standards, including Conventions not ratified by Ukraine ;Source of information: Website of the Confederation of Employers of Ukraine: http://employers.org.ua/media/Gender_Guidance_Confederation_ukr.pdf 

Prior to the current biennium, the ILO supported a gender audit for the Ministry of Social Policy. Based on and motivated by the audit the Ministry decided to prepare recommendations on gender audits for private companies and the ILO, during the present biennium, through support from ITC-ILO and the Project on Improvement of Wages System in Ukraine - provided technical advice including through sharing international best practices on gender equality.
Source of information: [Order of the Ministry of Social Policy of Ukraine No. 448 as of 09 August 2021: https://www.msp.gov.ua/documents/6081.html</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">6.2.1. Number of member States with policies to promote substantive equality of opportunity and treatment between women and men in the world of work, and strategies for their implementation</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.4. Increased capacity of ILO constituents to strengthen legislation, polices and measures to ensure equal opportunities and treatment in the world of work for persons with disabilities and other persons in vulnerable situations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Programme on HIV Prevention in the World and non-Discrimination based on HIV status among members of the National Agricultural Workers&apos; Union adopted in 2021. The ILO provided technical assistance to the Women&apos;s Committee of the Agricultural Workers&apos; Union of Ukraine in formulating clauses of the Union&apos;s Programme on HIV; and training to 17 trade union leaders from selected enterprises in food/agriculture sector on addressing stigma and discrimination in the world of work and on development of non-discrimination workplace policies and programmes. The Office conducted a survey in five enterprises of food sector to assess situations with violence and harassment and HIV stigma and discrimination in the world of work (2021). Follow-up training workshops were then organized for trade union leaders.

The Office also advocated for the adoption of HIV Programme for the Union; participated in the meeting of Women&apos;s Committee of the Union and spoke on HIV stigma and discrimination and importance to adopt non-discrimination policies and programmes.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">6.4.1. Number of member States that have measures to ensure equality of opportunities and treatment for persons with disabilities and for at least one of the following groups: indigenous or tribal peoples; ethnic minorities; persons living with HIV; or LGBTI persons</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop gender-responsive legislation, policies and measures for a world of work free from violence and harassment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promotion of gender equality and elimination of discrimination, violence and harassment in the world of work embedded in the draft collective agreement (signing &#x2013; tentatively May 2022).

Tripartite constituents identified legislative gaps for the ratification of C190. Ratification is prioritized by the Government of Ukraine for 2022. With RBSA support, 50 government officials and constituents capacitated by the means of trainings to promote collective bargaining (encompassing best practises and experience on elimination of violence harassment in the world of work). The ILO conducted a pre-ratification study and facilitated its tripartite validaion. 

The Office also supported a communication campaign of trade unions on elimination violence and harassment in the world of work (November &#x2013; December 2019) and contributed to the national UN campaign &quot;16 Days against gender-based violence&quot;.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">6.3.1. Number of member States that have reviewed relevant laws and policies in the light of Convention No. 190 and Recommendation No. 206, identified gaps, and taken measures to address them</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
